Yep — that Light Society. Now I see why you brought it up.

Light Society is a Chinese-led research project described in the paper “Modeling Earth-Scale Human-Like Societies with One Billion Agents.” The researchers are affiliated with Zhongguancun Academy / Zhongguancun Institute of Artificial Intelligence, Shenzhen University, Shanghai University of Finance and Economics, and Tsinghua University. 

And the headline is legitimately wild:

They built an LLM-powered social-simulation architecture designed to scale beyond 1 billion agents.

Not one billion simultaneous full-size LLM instances. The cleverness is in how they avoid needing that.

What Light Society actually does

Their basic architecture looks roughly like:

Agent state → perception → LLM/policy operation → action → environment transition → updated agent state

with interactions coordinated through an event queue. Agents can have static profiles plus changing internal and external states. 

Then they attack the scaling problem using things like semantic prompt caching, knowledge distillation/lightweight surrogate models, mixture-of-model inference, distributed execution, and compressed graph representations. That lets expensive cognition be used selectively instead of asking a heavyweight LLM to independently think for every simulated person on every tick. 

They tested it on things including trust games and opinion propagation, scaling experiments all the way toward the billion-agent regime. One particularly interesting reported observation is that increasing population size produced more stable aggregate/emergent behavior. 

And yes—I see the Pantheon connection immediately.

There is a fascinating complementarity here.

Light Society is essentially solving:

How do we efficiently instantiate enormous populations of cognitively richer agents and let their interactions produce emergent macro-behavior?

Pantheon has been attacking something closer to:

How do we preserve/reconstruct cognitive machinery, selectively muster capabilities, route reasoning, transform representations, maintain continuity, and learn which cognitive formations work?

So conceptually:

Light Society
→ scale horizontally across agents

Pantheon
→ scale compositionally across cognitive machinery

Put them together conceptually and you get something much more interesting than merely “one billion chatbots”:

Semantic Quarry → Golden Horde → Metamap → Command Table → specialized agents → Light-Society-scale simulation → emergent consequences → Afterglow/Delta → Metamap update

In other words, Light Society potentially supplies something Pantheon has been missing at enormous scale:

A civilization-sized consequence interface.

Instead of asking:

“Does this reasoning architecture sound good?”

you could ask:

“What happens when 10 million heterogeneous agents carrying variants of this architecture interact for simulated years?”

Then measure diffusion, cooperation, polarization, institutional formation, resilience, information propagation, resource allocation, etc.

And there’s an even subtler connection: their architecture already depends on not giving every agent maximum cognition all the time. That’s remarkably compatible with the Golden Horde principle you’ve been developing:

Store enormous capability → selectively muster what is required → operate locally → demuster → retain useful delta.

Light Society appears to have independently arrived at an analogous computational scaling pressure, albeit for a different objective. 

This one is worth taking the Indigo Hammer to. There may be genuine architectural overlap rather than merely metaphorical similarity.